WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:04.671
There's a phrase associated with
NASA: failure is not an option.

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:07.549
But there's another 
one in innovation

00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:10.468
 that is fail fast and fail often.

00:00:10.468 --> 00:00:11.469
It's a world of both.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:15.724
Iris is the first American
robot on the moon.

00:00:15.724 --> 00:00:17.434
It is the first university-built.

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:18.518
It is the first student-led.

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:20.812
The first for proving

00:00:20.812 --> 00:00:23.398
the capability of small rovers.

00:00:30.905 --> 00:00:33.700
This is like teaching 
a baby to walk.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.413
And before it gets to that million
critical steps on another planet,

00:00:38.413 --> 00:00:41.416
there'll be tens of 
millions here on earth.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.252
My name is Red Whittaker.

00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:46.796
I'm a professor of robotics.

00:00:46.796 --> 00:00:49.758
This is my 50th year 
at Carnegie Mellon.

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:54.179
I created the Iris idea.

00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:57.432
And from that produced
the very first course

00:00:57.432 --> 00:00:59.601
that engaged and 
enrolled students

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:03.271
and fostered it to and through 
where we are right now.

00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:04.314
Copy that.

00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:07.692
Looks like we're taking the rough
average of our estimates.

00:01:07.692 --> 00:01:10.111
I'd say, still recommend 
backward 15.

00:01:10.111 --> 00:01:10.737
Right.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.198
Requesting permission to move 
backwards 15 centimeters

00:01:13.198 --> 00:01:14.741
at five centimeters per second.

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:18.495
Iris is a shoebox on wheels.

00:01:18.495 --> 00:01:22.415
It is going to the moon to 
prove that small is capable.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:27.212
Iris is two kilograms, has very 
bottlecap-wheel design

00:01:27.212 --> 00:01:28.213
made out of carbon fiber.

00:01:28.213 --> 00:01:32.342
Very, very small compared 
to typical SUV-sized rovers.

00:01:40.141 --> 00:01:45.063
The technology that has built
Iris over the years has really

00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:49.484
stemmed from various projects
that date back to the ‘80s,

00:01:49.484 --> 00:01:52.987
when Red really became 
interested in space robotics

00:01:52.987 --> 00:01:54.239
 and lunar robotics.

00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:58.660
I formulated this notion 
of developing thinking

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.288
machines that work in the 
world and worlds beyond.

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:03.998
Never changed one word of it

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:06.668
and never ran out of 
anything great to do.

00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:09.671
The one that's left 
on the checklist is

00:02:09.671 --> 00:02:12.715
robots to worlds beyond.

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:24.185
It takes a few wealthy individuals
who are willing to invest

00:02:24.185 --> 00:02:26.396
their own time and money

00:02:26.396 --> 00:02:30.692
and who are excited about space
to start this social movement.

00:02:30.692 --> 00:02:32.735
It's generating the imagination.

00:02:32.735 --> 00:02:36.114
It's really bringing the 
excitement back into space.

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:39.159
It's opening doors 
and opportunities

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:41.578
that really weren't here before.

00:02:42.537 --> 00:02:45.999
One of the biggest things 
about this project is that it's

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:48.293
not a government entity.

00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:50.295
Because it's a student project,

00:02:50.295 --> 00:02:52.338
reaching out to the community

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:55.175
is such a huge part 
of this venture.

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:59.220
And so having the students
do a crowdfunding campaign,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.265
learning how that process works,

00:03:02.265 --> 00:03:05.226
it's something that is 
a really unique opportunity

00:03:05.226 --> 00:03:07.020
to bring everybody in and show

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.522
that anybody can 
make it to the moon.

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:13.109
It can be a group of 
students with a dream

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:15.361
and that one unified vision.

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.738
These students can do it.

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:21.117
My grand dream for the mission

00:03:21.117 --> 00:03:25.747
is that Iris will drive one meter,

00:03:25.747 --> 00:03:27.165
once it gets to the moon.

00:03:27.165 --> 00:03:29.417
Tel, this is flight on ops.

00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:30.251
Do you copy?

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:32.003
Copy.

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:34.464
Sys, this is flight. 
Do you copy?

00:03:34.464 --> 00:03:35.924
Copy.

00:03:35.924 --> 00:03:38.676
Proceed to navigate 
towards the NSS

00:03:38.676 --> 00:03:40.637
on the far side of the lander.

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:43.514
Iris request permission 
to deploy from the lander.

00:03:43.514 --> 00:03:47.894
The mission success 
comes from the rover

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:50.855
deploying, driving, 
taking a picture

00:03:50.855 --> 00:03:53.483
and sending a picture 
back to Earth.

00:03:53.483 --> 00:03:56.444
Even if things go 
wrong after that,

00:03:56.444 --> 00:03:58.738
it can do the basics and it shows

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:01.115
that in future 
iterations, it's possible.

00:04:01.115 --> 00:04:05.995
So many thousands of 
things have to be right

00:04:05.995 --> 00:04:08.706
to get that far.

00:04:08.706 --> 00:04:10.959
And of course, anything 
that goes wrong

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:12.710
can be a fatal glitch.

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:17.340
Every rover mission
has two landings.

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:21.552
There's one where the 
lander makes it to the moon.

00:04:22.804 --> 00:04:24.597
And the second, 
more important one

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:27.308
is when the rover 
gets to the surface.

00:04:27.308 --> 00:04:29.686
All the other rovers

00:04:29.686 --> 00:04:33.648
have had big ramps 
they could roll down

00:04:33.648 --> 00:04:37.402
 or mechanisms that would 
place them on the surface

00:04:37.402 --> 00:04:41.322
or for Mars, a long cable

00:04:41.322 --> 00:04:43.408
that lowers them 
right to the surface.

00:04:43.408 --> 00:04:47.620
This is the very first 
that will drop-deploy.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:48.705
When it's in the moon,

00:04:48.705 --> 00:04:52.500
it's released and it 
floats to the surface.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.714
Our hope is that it
lands on level, easy terrain.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:02.093
Other challenges are the 
customary ones from space:

00:05:02.093 --> 00:05:06.681
vacuum, heat, cold, 
radiation, violent shaking.

00:05:06.681 --> 00:05:08.516
Success is never forgone.

00:05:08.516 --> 00:05:10.476
And anything beyond

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:12.603
a little bit of driving
on the moon

00:05:12.603 --> 00:05:14.105
is all bonus.

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:21.112
We are a payload on 
Astrobotic’s Peregrine Lander.

00:05:21.112 --> 00:05:24.782
Which is then a payload 
on the ULA Vulcan rocket,

00:05:24.782 --> 00:05:26.409
the main rocket that will take it

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:27.952
to orbit and eventually the moon.

00:05:27.952 --> 00:05:30.163
Physically, Iris is complete.

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:33.750
Every mechanical thing,
every electronic thing,

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:37.837
every little iota of the 
physical parts are done.

00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:39.297
Iris is onboard the lander.

00:05:39.297 --> 00:05:41.632
Iris is ready to go to the moon.

00:05:41.632 --> 00:05:43.885
And what remains is to

00:05:43.885 --> 00:05:47.680
refine our procedures, our training, our team.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.641
It will be operating 
the robot right here,

00:05:50.641 --> 00:05:53.269
from this room, 
when it’s on the moon.

00:05:54.937 --> 00:05:57.774
Any rover is risky business.

00:05:57.774 --> 00:05:59.984
When it's Lean development,

00:05:59.984 --> 00:06:03.279
without the big money,
without the long duration,

00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:05.740
without the 
aerospace infrastructure,

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.409
without the deep, 
deep experience,

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:10.745
it's even riskier.

00:06:10.745 --> 00:06:13.915
And something wonderful
about working with students

00:06:13.915 --> 00:06:18.169
is that their threshold 
for risk is consistent

00:06:18.169 --> 00:06:21.839
with what had to be
to make Iris a reality.

00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:25.134
Over 300 students, 
almost 400 students

00:06:25.134 --> 00:06:29.972
have participated 
in this since 2017.

00:06:29.972 --> 00:06:33.101
The fact that it's students
volunteering their time

00:06:33.101 --> 00:06:34.477
or taking a class

00:06:34.477 --> 00:06:37.980
that they get to have that 
impact is just, it's extraordinary.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:40.733
These students are going to be

00:06:40.733 --> 00:06:43.361
going out and being 
the next generation

00:06:43.361 --> 00:06:47.365
in the space industry
and bringing this experience

00:06:47.365 --> 00:06:51.327
to the next rover or 
to the next rocket,

00:06:51.327 --> 00:06:53.746
to the next world that we go to.

00:06:54.705 --> 00:06:59.502
They are the ones that 
have brought the creativity,

00:06:59.502 --> 00:07:03.506
the courage, the drive,
the indefatigable work

00:07:03.506 --> 00:07:05.216
and the pursuit of excellence

00:07:05.216 --> 00:07:06.592
and have made it possible.

